Sciatica
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Unilateral radiating pain below knee, Positive straight leg raise, Dermatomal sensory/motor deficit

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-041
Reason for Visit: Acute radicular pain crisis severely limiting mobility and causing significant distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported severe, unremitting unilateral leg pain with a sudden inability to weight-bear on the affected side. Urgent in-person assessment is mandated to safely exclude cauda equina syndrome, evaluate acute motor deficits, and provide immediate, potent analgesia before the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "A knife is stabbing from my lower back all the way down to my right calf, and I can't put any weight on my leg."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with unilateral, sharp, severe, and lancinating pain radiating from the lower back directly down the posterior and lateral right leg. The pain explicitly travels distinctly below the knee, extending into the calf and lateral ankle. The onset was sudden yesterday after lifting a heavy object. The patient reports a stepwise worsening of symptoms: initially a dull lower back ache, rapidly progressing over 12 hours to severe radiating pain directly provoked by sitting, coughing, sneezing, or forceful Valsalva maneuvers. Distinct paresthesia and burning numbness are strictly localized to the precise L5 dermatome. The patient notes objective motor weakness strictly corresponding to the affected nerve root, distinctly causing a mild right-sided foot drop when attempting to walk.
Functional Status: Bedbound; acutely unable to ambulate due to severe pain and weakness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to access the bathroom safely; high fall risk due to foot drop.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 mmHg (elevated secondary to pain) | HR: 92 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, lying supine with hips and knees flexed, intensely guarding the right leg.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination reveals a positive straight leg raise test on the right, provoking severe radicular leg pain precisely at 40 degrees of passive hip flexion. A distinct, compensatory functional list of the lumbar spine away from the symptomatic side is noted when standing is briefly attempted. The patient exhibits unilateral radiating pain below the knee and a clear dermatomal sensory/motor deficit. Sensation to pinprick is diminished over the right lateral calf and dorsum of the foot (L5 dermatome). Motor testing shows weakness in right extensor hallucis longus (power 4-/5). Deep tendon reflexes are symmetrical at the knees and ankles. Perianal sensation and anal sphincter tone are strictly intact; no urinary retention or incontinence.
Environmental Observations: Patient resting on a firm mattress on the floor; trip hazards (cords) present near the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Over-the-counter ibuprofen 400mg taken 4 hours ago with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Lumbar Radiculopathy (Sciatica - Right L5) - Status: Acute Severe Pain Exacerbation
Differentials:

Cauda Equina Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intact perianal sensation, normal anal tone, and absence of bilateral severe motor weakness or acute urinary retention.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Unilateral radiating pain provoked by neural tension maneuvers (straight leg raise) rather than a constant deep calf ache, and no localized swelling or erythema.

Piriformis Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of objective L5 motor weakness (extensor hallucis longus) and pain exacerbated by Valsalva maneuvers, strongly indicating a spinal root rather than peripheral entrapment.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe radicular pain crisis secondary to a suspected acute lumbar disc herniation. The severity of the pain, the new onset of subjective and objective weakness (foot drop), and the inability to mobilize triggered immediate concern for a structural neurological emergency. Deferring assessment would result in agonizing, unmanaged pain and leave the critical question of cauda equina syndrome unanswered. Urgent after-hours physical examination definitively ruled out central cord/cauda equina compression, allowing for safe, aggressive outpatient pharmacological management and immediate biomechanical offloading strategies, satisfying MBS criteria for urgent care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Tapentadol 50mg orally STAT and PRN for severe breakthrough pain. Prescribed Pregabalin 75mg at night for radicular nerve pain. Advised short-term relative rest in a position of comfort (psoas position).
Positive Behavioral Change:

Step 1: By resting flat on the back with pillows firmly supporting the knees, the patient mechanically flattens the lumbar spine, immediately opening the nerve foramina and relieving the acute root compression.

Step 2: By strictly avoiding sitting in low, soft chairs, the patient prevents excessive flexion of the lumbar spine, directly avoiding massive spikes in intradiscal pressure and subsequent nerve irritation.

Step 3: By taking the Pregabalin precisely two hours before bed, the patient proactively stabilizes the hyperactive nerve root, ensuring a continuous block of restorative sleep necessary for acute healing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to musculoskeletal physiotherapy once the acute pain crisis subsides.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 3 days to monitor motor weakness and arrange lumbar MRI if the foot drop progresses.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed immediately to the Emergency Department if you develop numbness between your legs (saddle area), lose control of your bladder or bowels, or if the foot weakness rapidly worsens to a complete inability to lift the toes.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; an MRI is only required if surgery is contemplated or "red flag" symptoms develop.
Clinical Course: The acute, agonizing pain will begin to dull within 48-72 hours with the strong medications, but the nerve irritation and mild weakness will likely take 4-8 weeks to fully resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for urgent review if the pain completely stops responding to the new medications or if the numbness spreads up the other leg.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A spinal disc has bulged and is directly crushing the main nerve running down your leg. The severe pain is the nerve firing continuously. The new medications include a strong painkiller and a specific drug to numb the angry nerve. You must keep the spine perfectly neutral to let the inflammation shrink.
Actionable Steps: Rest in the knees-bent position on a firm surface. Do not bend forward or lift anything. Take the medications exactly as prescribed.

